// test-execution
// test-no-warnings
// #1450 box 4: `<string.h>` is decided by TYPE REGISTRATION, which runs before
// any text is generated — not by the emitters that happen to need it.
//
// WHAT THIS PINS. Six render-time raisers used to re-assert `needsString`:
// four in `StringHandlers` and two in `StringDeclHelper`. Every one was
// redundant — `registerAllVariableTypes` had already settled the flag — so
// removing them changed nothing across 1249 fixtures. But "the corpus cannot
// tell them apart" is not "they are redundant", and the case that separates
// them was missing: a string PARAMETER with no local `string<N>` declaration
// anywhere in the file.
//
// If registration did not see parameters, this file would declare no string
// type, the emitters would be the only requirers, and `strncpy` would be
// emitted with no `<string.h>` — a clean transpile and a compile error. It
// does see them, and this fixture is what says so.
//
// The no-warnings marker matters here: the failure is a missing declaration,
// which is a WARNING in C89-era compilers and an error under `-Werror`, so the
// marker is what turns it into a test failure rather than a silent pass.
//
// `main` is the negative control: it declares nothing and copies nothing, so a
// change that required `<string.h>` unconditionally would not be caught by the
// assertions alone.
void setIt(string<8> s) {
    s <- "hi";
}

u32 main() {
    string<8> local <- "ab";
    setIt(local);
    if (local.char_count != 2) return 1;
    return 0;
}
